    {
      "id": "syn_interpretive_questions_102059b5c5ae",
      "kind": "interpretive_questions",
      "facts": [
        "The letters address assemblies that included named households, enslaved and free people, patrons, workers, litigants, hungry people, and participants with unequal resources and vulnerability.",
        "The evidence keeps calling, freedom, bodily belonging, meal humiliation, and household service within a slaveholding and economically unequal society without turning description into endorsement.",
        "Excavated Corinth preserves a Roman forum, roads, basilicas, shops, fountains, temples, theater, inscriptions, and administrative monuments within a refounded colony and provincial capital.",
        "Public hierarchy, adjudication, honor, commerce, and religious visibility form concrete context for status competition and lawsuits without identifying a faction or courtroom in First Corinthians."
      ],
      "verse_refs": [
        "1 Corinthians 1:10-31",
        "1 Corinthians 1:26-31"
